---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Security Architecture & Threat Model">
  {/* Header */}
  <section class="pt-20 pb-12 px-6 max-w-7xl mx-auto text-center ambient-glow">
    <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3.5 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#1A1817] border border-[#A47764]/40 text-[#A47764] text-xs font-mono font-medium mb-6">
      <span>Frozen Specification &bull; Sections 0, 12, 15</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-6xl font-extrabold text-[#F0EEE9] tracking-tight">
      Zero-Trust Cryptographic Architecture
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base sm:text-lg text-[#C5BFB8] max-w-2xl mx-auto">
      BYOS is engineered around mathematical guarantees, not corporate promises. Persistent mailbox storage is segregated from key custody.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* Visual Cryptographic Hierarchy Diagram */}
  <section class="py-8 px-6 max-w-7xl mx-auto">
    <div class="p-8 rounded-2xl border border-[#4A4A4A] bg-[#1A1817] shadow-2xl">
      <div class="text-xs font-mono text-[#A47764] font-semibold">SECTION 12 &bull; KEY DERIVATION HIERARCHY</div>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-bold text-[#F0EEE9]">The Seed-to-Mailbox Cryptographic Tree</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-[#C5BFB8] max-w-2xl">
        A single offline 24-word BIP39 mnemonic deterministically generates all cryptographic capabilities without ever transmitting the private root to the server.
      </p>

      {/* Visual Tree */}
      <div class="mt-8 grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4">
        {/* Node 1 */}
        <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-[#23201E] border border-[#A47764] flex flex-col justify-between">
          <div>
            <div class="text-[10px] font-mono text-[#A47764]">PRIMARY ENTROPY</div>
            <div class="mt-1 font-bold text-sm text-[#F0EEE9]">24-Word BIP39 Seed</div>
            <p class="mt-2 text-[11px] text-[#C5BFB8]">
              256-bit cryptographic entropy stored offline on paper or hardware token.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="mt-4 text-[10px] font-mono text-[#6E6E6E] pt-2 border-t border-[#383431]">
            Client-Only Custody
          </div>
        </div>

        {/* Node 2 */}
        <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-[#23201E] border border-[#383431] flex flex-col justify-between">
          <div>
            <div class="text-[10px] font-mono text-[#A47764]">DERIVATION STEP</div>
            <div class="mt-1 font-bold text-sm text-[#F0EEE9]">Root Secret (HKDF)</div>
            <p class="mt-2 text-[11px] text-[#C5BFB8]">
              HKDF-SHA256 derivation with domain separation tag <code>&quot;byos-v1-root&quot;</code>.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="mt-4 text-[10px] font-mono text-[#6E6E6E] pt-2 border-t border-[#383431]">
            RFC 5869 KDF
          </div>
        </div>

        {/* Node 3 */}
        <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-[#23201E] border border-emerald-500/40 flex flex-col justify-between">
          <div>
            <div class="text-[10px] font-mono text-emerald-400">MAILBOX ENCRYPTION</div>
            <div class="mt-1 font-bold text-sm text-[#F0EEE9]">X25519 HPKE Keypair</div>
            <p class="mt-2 text-[11px] text-[#C5BFB8]">
              Public key encrypts inbound mail payloads; private key unwraps in-memory on client.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="mt-4 text-[10px] font-mono text-emerald-400 pt-2 border-t border-[#383431]">
            RFC 9180 Standard
          </div>
        </div>

        {/* Node 4 */}
        <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-[#23201E] border border-[#383431] flex flex-col justify-between">
          <div>
            <div class="text-[10px] font-mono text-[#A47764]">ACCOUNT RECOVERY</div>
            <div class="mt-1 font-bold text-sm text-[#F0EEE9]">Ed25519 Auth Keypair</div>
            <p class="mt-2 text-[11px] text-[#C5BFB8]">
              Signs server challenge-response tokens during account recovery and root rotation.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="mt-4 text-[10px] font-mono text-[#6E6E6E] pt-2 border-t border-[#383431]">
            RFC 8032 Signature
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* Threat Scenarios Matrix */}
  <section class="py-12 px-6 max-w-7xl mx-auto">
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#F0EEE9] mb-6">Threat Scenarios &amp; Cryptographic Mitigations</h2>
    
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
      {/* Scenario 1 */}
      <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#383431] bg-[#1A1817]">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <span class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-rose-950 border border-rose-800 text-rose-400 text-xs font-mono font-bold">THREAT 01</span>
          <h3 class="font-bold text-base text-[#F0EEE9]">Total Control-Plane Database Leak</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-xs text-[#C5BFB8]">
          <p><strong>Attacker Capability:</strong> Full read access to PostgreSQL tables, API sessions, and routing records.</p>
          <p class="text-emerald-400"><strong>Mitigation Guarantee:</strong> Zero email bodies or attachments are stored in the database. All mailbox keys are stored as wrapped ciphertexts. An attacker gains only metadata and blind HMAC search tokens.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/* Scenario 2 */}
      <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#383431] bg-[#1A1817]">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <span class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-rose-950 border border-rose-800 text-rose-400 text-xs font-mono font-bold">THREAT 02</span>
          <h3 class="font-bold text-base text-[#F0EEE9]">Customer S3 Bucket Compromise</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-xs text-[#C5BFB8]">
          <p><strong>Attacker Capability:</strong> Direct unauthorized access to the customer&apos;s AWS S3 or MinIO bucket.</p>
          <p class="text-emerald-400"><strong>Mitigation Guarantee:</strong> Every object is an AES-256-GCM ciphertext encrypted with a random content key sealed under the recipient&apos;s HPKE public key. Without the offline client private key, the objects cannot be decrypted.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/* Scenario 3 */}
      <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#383431] bg-[#1A1817]">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <span class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-rose-950 border border-rose-800 text-rose-400 text-xs font-mono font-bold">THREAT 03</span>
          <h3 class="font-bold text-base text-[#F0EEE9]">Stolen Laptop or Lost Client Device</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-xs text-[#C5BFB8]">
          <p><strong>Attacker Capability:</strong> Physical possession of an employee&apos;s workstation.</p>
          <p class="text-emerald-400"><strong>Mitigation Guarantee:</strong> Admin or user triggers instant session, device grant, and bridge token revocation. The user rotates their root secret using their 24-word seed, rendering cached tokens useless.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/* Scenario 4 */}
      <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#383431] bg-[#1A1817]">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <span class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-rose-950 border border-rose-800 text-rose-400 text-xs font-mono font-bold">THREAT 04</span>
          <h3 class="font-bold text-base text-[#F0EEE9]">Rogue Cloud / BYOS Infrastructure Admin</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-xs text-[#C5BFB8]">
          <p><strong>Attacker Capability:</strong> A rogue BYOS operator attempting to inspect customer mailboxes.</p>
          <p class="text-emerald-400"><strong>Mitigation Guarantee:</strong> Zero master decryption keys exist in the BYOS architecture. Inbound mail is encrypted transiently before upload to customer storage. The operator cannot decrypt historical mail.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* RFC Standards Table */}
  <section class="py-12 px-6 max-w-7xl mx-auto">
    <div class="p-8 rounded-2xl border border-[#383431] bg-[#1A1817]">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#F0EEE9]">Frozen RFC Cryptographic Standards</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-[#C5BFB8]">
        We do not invent cryptographic protocols. Every primitive is verified against official RFC test vectors.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 text-xs">
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-[#23201E] border border-[#383431]">
          <div class="font-mono text-[#A47764] font-bold">RFC 9180</div>
          <div class="font-bold text-[#F0EEE9] mt-1">Hybrid Public Key Encryption</div>
          <div class="text-[#6E6E6E] text-[11px] mt-1">DHKEM(X25519, HKDF-SHA256) + AES-256-GCM</div>
        </div>

        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-[#23201E] border border-[#383431]">
          <div class="font-mono text-[#A47764] font-bold">RFC 8032</div>
          <div class="font-bold text-[#F0EEE9] mt-1">Edwards-Curve Signatures</div>
          <div class="text-[#6E6E6E] text-[11px] mt-1">Ed25519 for account recovery challenge-verify</div>
        </div>

        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-[#23201E] border border-[#383431]">
          <div class="font-mono text-[#A47764] font-bold">RFC 6376</div>
          <div class="font-bold text-[#F0EEE9] mt-1">DomainKeys Identified Mail</div>
          <div class="text-[#6E6E6E] text-[11px] mt-1">RSA-2048 outbound cryptographic signing</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
